t a new region  and if you know you repeat this a few  more times we'd likely down to drive the  company down on your endless operational  toil or be forced to turn down business  opportunities by not building new  regions at all so you know we had to  just find the Goldilocks balance choose  the right work  you know there were things that we could  have chosen not to do but we tried to  find you know the right level of changes  to accomplish our goal so with all of  that said we had an execution plan for  this project this is you know purely  like design this is what we want to see  now you know here's where the rubber  hits the road how do we actually get  this done what we hoped was we would do  discovery of the IBM environment of our  own systems figure out what we needed to  do and that's basically where all the  stuff I just talked about came from then  we would document all of that in  broadcast it was time to do the work  teams would do this work in parallel  they would do some repeated test build  outs find problems repeat and finally  stand up a for-real environment and  leave it running then we test that and  then we ship it to our customers and the  hope here was that you know we could  time box this work  keep team's involvement relatively  constrained to just a couple of  milestones containerization and one  targeting testing the new region and it  but in between those two we wanted to  ensure that teams could and could and  would do other things for example  earlier this year we ship distributed  tracing support which required a whole  lot of teams to do a whole lot of work  and if we had said okay you know all of  that can't happen because backpack we  would have been in trouble this project  would not have happened that said that's  great and all but I wouldn't be standing  up here in front of you if the project  had gone perfectly or you know maybe I  would be but honestly I think that that  would be pretty boring because none of  the things we did technically are all  that unusual maybe the service discovery  thing is a little bit unusual but you  know what's more interesting to me is  that we made some change the changes at  some scale so I'm going to go over some  lessons that we learned along the way  and some each once each one up with a  set of things that we'd start stop and  continue in future large-scale projects  and I hope that these are all lessons  that can be relevant to you as you  consider large-scale projects of your  own so first quick ramp ups how do you  prioritize the work the backpack project  was predicated on having support from  the business because we needed basically  every team at the company to do work for  us so we needed that work to get on  their roadmap so let's talk about how  Road mapping works in New Relic  first it's important to note that every  engineering team at New Relic is  autonomous that means that they have  their own roadmaps which is set by the  team and their product manager to meet  both the team's own goals as well as to  meet the broader requirements of the  business those broader requirements come  from a group known as the product  council which every quarter or so  produces a list of the top five  high-priority  projects in the company in order of  priority and teams are supposed to  contribute to those projects if they  can't and some of the projects might be  something that only a single team can do  and what that means is that other teams  just need to make sure that that team is  not blocked they can get their job done  and for large projects like backpack it  means that the work for the project  preempts the rest of the teams roadmap  now this project actually does work but  there's a catch what happens if a  project needs resources to move forward  but the product Council for whatever  reason hasn't prioritized the project  yet that's what happened with that pack  there was a month or so of delay where  you know we thought we were ready to go  we needed teams to do work clock was  ticking and it wasn't prioritized and  then suddenly all of a sudden we were  prioritized almost all teams were  knocking at our door and like this  roller coaster at California Adventure  in Los Angeles where I grew up this  roller coaster has a fast start if you  don't know what's coming you're gonna  smack your head and that's kind of what  happened to us in retrospect this sink  swing from no support to near total  support just wasn't good for the project  we had had to transition from operating  relatively independently you know doing  all this discovery work doing some  build-out in the environment to having  every team at the company knocking on  our door trying to figure out what the  hell they had to do and the discovery  worked there was well organized you know  there was a process for having meetings  but even still it left the backpack  engineering team scrambling with our  sudden success in getting attention you  know we just weren't ready we didn't  have full documentation of what we were  looking for service discovery tooling  wasn't ready court ruling wasn't ready  and most importantly we just didn't have  an easily digestible philosophy of what  we were actually asking people to do how  to think about the problem space we were  in if engineers had understand how we  were thinking about this how we were  thinking about the problem better they  could have made better decisions along  the way there would have been less  thrash and we didn't have that clearly  articulated we'd articulated it in  pieces along the way but not in one  place where people would go to  understand so in the future we're gonna  start preparing for what happens when a  project actually gets that high priority  we're gonna produce a project philosophy  document and we're going to keep it  updated if things change and we're gonna  continue prioritizing important work  across the company because that is  useful relatedly  the biggest problem we ran 