atch  up without having to catch up on you  know four weeks of blog posts so in the  future got to have that potentialized  documentation having better linting  would have helped a ton but we're gonna  continue to blog internally it's really  useful for engineers to know what's  actually going on at the company and  have a more flat organizational  structure as a result of that and you  know we're gonna continue to communicate  it using as many channels as possible  because we know that one just does just  one doesn't work next local maximums one  consequence of our incomplete  standardization was that teams were just  heavily incentivized to make the system  better locally so what I mean by that is  let's take a thought exercise imagine  that you're on a team three years ago a  new relic and you managed say 20  services it's all a part of one system  and you want some things  that the platform doesn't provide you  you want chain to place from service to  service or from stage into production  you want some kind of shared  configuration and you want service  discovery and so you build all these  things and for three years you get the  benefit of it before the company  provides them to you as part of standard  that's tooling and now someone comes  along and says ok as part of this  project we need you to start using the  standardized tooling because we need you  to be on our mesas platform and that has  only one door in it's not gonna be great  you're gonna have some kind of  transition pain but the promise is that  after you're on standardized tool and  you're just gonna be in much better  shape but the reality is that you're not  really gonna be there's gonna be some  aspect of the standardized tooling  that's almost certainly worse than what  you built for yourself because it's  solving a more brought a more general  problem not what you actually intended  to solve and this isn't a good way to  make friends is if you know they're a  bunch of teams that have done t